# Code owners for context-ledger.
#
# GitHub reads this file from the repository root (also honoured at .github/ or
# docs/). Every pattern below requests a review from the named owner when a
# matching path changes. It is a routing rule, not an access control: it only
# bites once branch protection on `main` requires review from Code Owners.
#
# Syntax is gitignore-style patterns, last matching rule wins.

# Default owner for everything not claimed more specifically below.
*                           @ansh-n-chovatiya

# --- The parts where a bad change is quiet rather than loud -----------------

# What CI executes, and with what token scope. An unpinned action or a widened
# `permissions:` block is a supply-chain change wearing a config diff's clothes.
/.github/                   @ansh-n-chovatiya

# The governance statements themselves. SECURITY.md promises a disclosure
# channel and a response window to people outside this repository.
/SECURITY.md                @ansh-n-chovatiya
/CODEOWNERS                 @ansh-n-chovatiya
/LICENSE                    @ansh-n-chovatiya

# The tool's own trust and gate machinery: the code that decides whether a
# command arriving as data is allowed to run.
/ctx/                       @ansh-n-chovatiya

# The executables a user invokes, including the Windows wrapper whose exit-code
# propagation is easy to break and hard to notice.
/bin/                       @ansh-n-chovatiya

# Hooks and commands run inside a user's session.
/hooks/                     @ansh-n-chovatiya
/commands/                  @ansh-n-chovatiya
/agents/                    @ansh-n-chovatiya
/skills/                    @ansh-n-chovatiya

# The suite is the only thing standing behind every claim above.
/tests/                     @ansh-n-chovatiya

# Release identity: the version here and in ctx/__init__.py must agree, and CI
# checks that they do.
/.claude-plugin/            @ansh-n-chovatiya
